LINUX BASICS - SESSION 2

FILE AND DIRECTORY MANAGEMENT

The symbol / is the root of the Linux file system.

pwd
Shows the current working directory.

whoami
Shows the name of the current user.

uname -a
Shows information about the Linux system.

mkdir foldername
Creates a new directory.

rmdir foldername
Removes an empty directory.

rm -r foldername
Removes a directory and its contents.
Use this command carefully.

CREATING AND MANAGING FILES

touch filename
Creates a new empty file.

rm filename
Removes a file.

cp source destination
Copies a file.

Example:
cp demo.txt demo-copy.txt

mv oldname newname
Renames a file or moves it to another directory.

Example:
mv old.txt new.txt

FILE VIEWING COMMANDS

cat filename
Displays the complete contents of a file.

less filename
Opens a file for scrolling.

Inside less:
Space - move down one page
b - move up one page
q - quit

Install vim and less on Debian or Ubuntu:

sudo apt install vim less

VI AND VIM

vi is a text editor. Vim is an improved version of vi.

Open a file with vim:

vim filename

Vim has three important modes:

Normal mode
Used for moving around the file and running shortcuts.
Press Escape to return to normal mode.

Insert mode
Used for typing text.
Press i, a, o, or one of the other insert commands.

Command mode
Used for saving, quitting, searching, and going to a line.
Press : from normal mode.

IMPORTANT VI SHORTCUTS

Moving around in normal mode:

h - move left
j - move down
k - move up
l - move right
0 - move to the beginning of the line
$ - move to the end of the line
gg - go to the first line
G - go to the last line
:number - go to a specific line

Examples:
:1       go to line 1
:500     go to line 500

Starting to type:

i - insert before the cursor
a - insert after the cursor
I - insert at the beginning of the line
A - insert at the end of the line
o - open a new line below the current line
O - open a new line above the current line

Deleting and changing text:

x - delete the character under the cursor
dd - delete the current line
dw - delete the word under the cursor
D - delete from the cursor to the end of the line
u - undo the last change
Ctrl+r - redo a change

Copying and pasting:

yy - copy the current line
nyy - copy n lines, for example 3yy
p - paste after the cursor
P - paste before the cursor

Searching:

/word - search forward for a word
?word - search backward for a word
n - go to the next match
N - go to the previous match

Saving and quitting:

:w - save the file
:q - quit vi
:wq - save and quit
:q! - quit without saving
ZZ - save and quit from normal mode

Example editing sequence:

1. Open a file:
	vim demo.txt
2. Press i and type your changes.
3. Press Escape to return to normal mode.
4. Type :wq and press Enter to save and quit.

VIEWING THE START AND END OF FILES

head -20 demo.txt
Displays the first 20 lines of a file.

tail -20 demo.txt
Displays the last 20 lines of a file.

tail -f demo.txt
Continuously displays new lines added to a file. This is useful for watching log files.
Press Ctrl+c to stop it.

USING ECHO WITH FILES

echo "hello" > demo.txt
Writes hello to the file and replaces the old contents.

echo "hello" >> demo.txt
Adds hello to the end of the file without deleting the old contents.

QUICK RECAP

File commands: mkdir, rmdir, rm, touch, cp, and mv
Viewing commands: cat, less, head, and tail
Vim modes: normal mode, insert mode, and command mode
Save and quit: :wq
Quit without saving: :q!